  <!-- FABRIC-SPECIFIC GUIDE -->
  <section class="sc-section" aria-labelledby="fabric-guide-heading">
    <div class="sc-wrap">
      <div class="sc-section-head sc-section-head--center">
        <span class="sc-tag">Fabric Guide</span>
        <h2 class="sc-h2" id="fabric-guide-heading">Shrinkage Behaviour by Fabric Type</h2>
        <p class="sc-lead sc-lead--center">Understanding why each fabric shrinks the way it does helps production teams make better pre-production decisions — before a misfitted sample costs time and material.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-fabric-grid">
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Cotton</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Highest natural-fibre shrinkage risk</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 3–5%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 2–4%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Knit up to 8% warp</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">Cotton fibres absorb water and swell significantly, increasing yarn diameter and reducing yarn length. The warp direction always shrinks more than the weft because warp yarns carry the tension of weaving. Knit constructions show higher shrinkage than wovens because the looped structure has more room to contract. Sanforizing during finishing reduces residual shrinkage to under 1% for woven cotton, but unsanforized fabric must be accounted for in pattern making.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Polyester</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Lowest wash shrinkage, heat-set dependent</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 1–2%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 0.5–1.5%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Total under 3%</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">Polyester is hydrophobic — it does not absorb water, so wash-induced swelling shrinkage is negligible. The primary risk is thermal shrinkage: if fabric is not properly heat-set during finishing, high dryer temperatures cause fibre relaxation. Properly heat-set polyester is among the most dimensionally stable fabrics in production. Blending with cotton raises shrinkage toward cotton's range proportionally.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Wool</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Felting risk on top of dimensional shrinkage</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 4–6%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 3–5%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Felt risk above 40°C</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">Wool has two types of shrinkage: dimensional shrinkage from fibre swelling, and felting from heat plus mechanical agitation. Felting is irreversible — scales on wool fibres interlock permanently when agitated in hot water. Machine washable wool uses chlorine-Hercosett or Optim fibre treatment to remove the scales. Always specify gentle cycle and 30°C maximum for unlabeled wool to avoid both shrinkage types during testing.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Viscose / Rayon</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Highest shrinkage of any common fabric</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 4–7%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 3–6%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Total up to 12%</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">Viscose fibres lose up to 30% of their tensile strength when wet and swell significantly. This makes viscose the highest-shrinkage common fabric — warp shrinkage of 7% or more is not unusual in untreated fabric. Additionally, wet viscose distorts easily under its own weight when hanging to dry, adding directional shrinkage. Pattern allowances for viscose need to be built conservatively, and buyer tolerance agreements should reflect the material's inherent behaviour.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Cotton-Elastane Blends</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Shrinkage driven by cotton, restricted by elastane</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 4–6%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 3–5%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Heat risk from elastane</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">In cotton-elastane blends, the cotton component drives most of the wash shrinkage, while the elastane component provides elastic recovery. However, elastane degrades permanently at tumble-dry heat, which reduces recovery over time and changes the dimensional behaviour. Always test at the care conditions the garment will carry on its label — the interaction between cotton shrinkage and elastane recovery means test conditions significantly affect results.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="sc-fabric-card">
          <p class="sc-fabric-name">Denim</p>
          <h3 class="sc-fabric-title">Controlled by construction density and finishing</h3>
          <div class="sc-fabric-stats">
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Warp 3–5%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Weft 2–4%</span>
            <span class="sc-stat-pill">Sanforized or raw</span>
          </div>
          <p class="sc-fabric-text">Raw denim shrinks significantly on first wash — 5–10% is normal and is considered part of the product experience in premium markets. Sanforized denim has already been mechanically pre-shrunk and shows residual shrinkage under 1–2%. For production, always confirm whether fabric is sanforized and adjust pattern allowances accordingly. Stone-washed and enzyme-washed denim has already been through multiple wet processes and shows minimal further shrinkage.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="sc-divider" />

  <!-- HOW SHRINKAGE IS MEASURED -->
  <section class="sc-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
    <div class="sc-wrap">
      <div class="sc-section-head sc-section-head--center">
        <span class="sc-tag">Test Method</span>
        <h2 class="sc-h2" id="method-heading">How Fabric Shrinkage Is Measured in Production</h2>
        <p class="sc-lead sc-lead--center">Understanding the test procedure helps production teams accurately interpret lab results and apply them correctly to pattern making.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-steps">
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">01 — Sample Preparation</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Mark and measure the fabric</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">Cut a fabric sample at least 50cm × 50cm per ISO 3759. Mark benchmark points at a precise distance — typically 35cm apart in both warp and weft directions — using indelible ink or thread. Record exact measurements before washing. Condition the sample at 20°C and 65% relative humidity for 4 hours before marking.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">02 — Washing and Drying</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Wash under defined conditions</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">Wash at the specified temperature and cycle type per AATCC 135 or ISO 6330. Use a standard detergent at the specified concentration. Complete 5 cycles for standard buyer testing or the number specified in the tech pack. Dry using the method matching the garment's care label — tumble, line, or flat.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">03 — Measure and Calculate</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Remeasure and apply the formula</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">After drying, condition the sample again at 20°C / 65% RH for 4 hours. Remeasure the benchmark distances. Calculate shrinkage as: (original − final) ÷ original × 100. A negative result indicates growth rather than shrinkage. Report warp and weft separately. Three samples per fabric lot is the minimum for statistically valid results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">04 — Pattern Allowance</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Apply allowances to cutting patterns</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">Divide the finished garment measurement by (1 minus the shrinkage decimal) to get the cut length. For 5% warp shrinkage and a 60cm finished chest: 60 ÷ 0.95 = 63.16cm cut length. Apply warp and weft allowances independently — they are different values and affect length and width separately.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">05 — Pre-Shrink Treatment</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Reduce shrinkage before cutting</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">For high-risk fabrics, consider sanforizing (mechanical compressive shrinkage for cotton wovens), compax finishing (for knits), or heat setting (for polyester and nylon). These treatments reduce residual shrinkage to under 1–2% and allow tighter pattern tolerances, reducing fabric waste from over-generous allowances.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-step">
          <p class="sc-step-num">06 — Buyer Tolerance Check</p>
          <h3 class="sc-step-title">Verify against specification before bulk</h3>
          <p class="sc-step-text">Compare the calculated total shrinkage against the buyer's stated tolerance. Most standard buyers accept 5% total; premium buyers often specify 3%. If shrinkage exceeds tolerance, the fabric must be rejected or pre-treated before cutting. Never start bulk production without a passing shrinkage test result on the actual production fabric lot.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

