  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="sc-section" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
    <div class="sc-wrap">
      <div class="sc-section-head sc-section-head--center">
        <span class="sc-tag">FAQ</span>
        <h2 class="sc-h2" id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions About Fabric Shrinkage</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-faq-list">
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is fabric shrinkage and why does it happen?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Fabric shrinkage is the permanent reduction in fabric dimensions after washing or drying. It happens because fibres absorb water and swell, increasing in diameter and shortening in length. Yarns also release the tension built up during spinning, weaving, and finishing processes — called relaxation shrinkage — which happens primarily on the first wash. Heat accelerates both mechanisms, which is why higher wash temperatures always produce more shrinkage for natural fibres.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the difference between warp and weft shrinkage?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Warp shrinkage is the reduction along the length of the fabric, parallel to the selvage. Weft shrinkage is the reduction across the width. Warp shrinkage is almost always higher because warp yarns carry the tension of weaving and contain more residual stress. In pattern making, warp shrinkage affects the length of garment panels — bodice length, sleeve length, trouser inseam — while weft shrinkage affects width — chest circumference, hip width, sleeve width.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is relaxation shrinkage and how is it different from other types?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Relaxation shrinkage occurs when the internal stresses built up during spinning, weaving, and finishing are released on first contact with water. It happens regardless of wash temperature and is largely irreversible — the fabric settles into its natural, unstressed state. It is different from hygral expansion (shrinkage from fibre swelling with water) and thermal shrinkage (shrinkage from heat during drying). In practice, most of the total measured shrinkage in cotton wovens is a combination of relaxation and hygral shrinkage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What is the standard test method for measuring fabric shrinkage?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>The main standards are AATCC 135 (used primarily in the USA), ISO 6330, and ISO 3759. Each specifies how to prepare fabric samples, what detergent and water hardness to use, wash machine type, temperature, cycle length, and drying method. AATCC 135 uses HE (high-efficiency) machine settings; ISO 6330 specifies front-loading machines. Shrinkage is calculated as: (original dimension minus final dimension) divided by original dimension, multiplied by 100. Most buyer QC specs require the AATCC 135 method for US market and ISO 6330 for EU market orders.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How does wash temperature affect shrinkage?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Higher wash temperatures increase shrinkage for natural fibres like cotton, wool, and linen because heat accelerates fibre swelling and speeds up the release of residual manufacturing stresses. Moving from 30°C to 60°C can increase cotton shrinkage by 30–50% compared to cold washing. For synthetics like polyester and nylon, wash temperature has minimal impact on shrinkage — their primary risk is high drying temperatures, not wash temperature. Always test at the temperature the garment's care label will specify.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How does drying method affect fabric shrinkage?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Tumble drying produces the highest shrinkage because it combines heat with mechanical agitation — both factors independently drive shrinkage. Line drying typically shows lower shrinkage than tumble drying, but hanging wet fabric under gravity can cause lengthwise stretching in heavy or loose constructions. Flat drying is the least distorting method, particularly important for knits and loosely constructed fabrics. For shrinkage testing, always use the drying method specified in the tech pack or that will appear on the garment's care label.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>What are acceptable shrinkage limits for garment production?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Standard buyer tolerance for most markets is 3–5% total shrinkage. Woven cotton buyers typically accept 3% warp and 3% weft for regular-fit garments. Premium buyers — particularly in the EU — often specify 2% for tailored or structured garments. Knit buyers generally accept 5–8% because knit constructions inherently shrink more. Terry and fleece accept higher tolerances of 8–10%. Always confirm the specific tolerance stated in the buyer's tech pack or material spec sheet before committing to production fabric.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How do I calculate cutting pattern allowance for shrinkage?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>The correct formula is: cut dimension = finished measurement ÷ (1 − shrinkage ÷ 100). For a 60cm finished chest width with 4% weft shrinkage, the cut width is 60 ÷ 0.96 = 62.5cm. Apply warp and weft allowances separately — they have different values and affect different dimensions of the pattern. Add a small test margin of 0.5–1% when dealing with a new fabric lot or supplier. This calculator computes both allowances automatically when you enter the initial measurement.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Can fabric shrinkage be prevented or reduced before production?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Sanforizing is the most common pre-shrinking treatment for woven cotton — a mechanical process that compresses the fabric along its length, reducing residual shrinkage to under 1%. For knits, the equivalent is compax finishing. Polyester and nylon are heat-set at high temperatures to stabilise their dimensions. Pre-washing fabric before cutting is an option for small production runs or bespoke garments. For commercial production, always specify whether you require sanforized or preshrunk fabric in your material purchase order to avoid surprises at the inspection stage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sc-faq-item">
          <button class="sc-faq-btn" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>How does GSM affect shrinkage in garment production?</span>
            <span class="sc-faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="sc-faq-answer">
            <p>Higher GSM fabrics generally shrink more than lower GSM fabrics of the same fibre type because they contain more yarn mass and therefore more residual stress. A 200 GSM cotton single jersey will typically shrink more than a 140 GSM jersey in the same fibre composition. GSM also affects the rate at which fabric releases stress — heavier fabrics may show more shrinkage across additional wash cycles, while lighter fabrics often complete most of their shrinkage in the first two cycles. This is why buyers for heavier knits typically allow wider shrinkage tolerances.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

